[#:seon.task{:name
             "a-graph-counts-its-messages-and-says-when-it-is-quiet",
             :plan
             "Reproduction (prototypes e6_quiescence, e7_one_system): with each message counted when it is promised (a step's outputs, an inject, a message an edge owes the proc) and uncounted when a step has consumed it, the count reaching 0 is exact quiescence, delivered as an event: quiet 1.5 ms after a 50-join burst; not quiet until an owed child exit arrived (1008 ms for sleep 1); a check run ends on it. The prototype (a step wrapper and an atom) cost 150-230 ns per step (estimate 50-100). Correct, in flow/impl.clj: the graph keeps an AtomicLong; send-outputs adds the step's output count before putting, the proc loop subtracts one after transform, inject adds its count, a state key ::flow/promised adds owed messages; the 0-transition puts the busy spell's number on a ::flow/quiet out (sliding 1). Quiet with a proc whose ping says :waiting is blocked, and the ping names why. Replaces: timeouts that end runs, settled? polling, and the reconciler's own quiet heuristic for the procs it does not see.",
             :kind :work,
             :proofs
             #{jolt.flow-test/quiet-arrives-exactly-when-the-last-message-is-consumed
               jolt.flow-test/an-owed-message-holds-quiet-off}}
 #:seon.task{:name "ping-says-where-a-proc-is-parked",
             :plan
             "Reproduction (prototype e6): a back-pressure deadlock (a join that reads only what it lacks, fed by one sequential producer) leaves every proc :running with frozen counts, 99 messages in flight and no quiet event; nothing names the wait-for cycle. Cause: the proc loop (flow/impl.clj proc run, send-outputs) parks either in alts!! over its read set or in alts!! putting one output, and ping reports neither. Correct: the proc keeps its park site in the loop ({:reading #{cids}} or {:writing coord}), and ping answers it as ::flow/parked. With `jolt.async/a-channel-shows-its-fill`, a wait-for graph (writing -> a full in-chan -> its reader's park site) finds the cycle, and the blocked entry names it.",
             :kind :work,
             :needs #{"jolt.async/a-channel-shows-its-fill"},
             :proofs
             #{jolt.flow-test/ping-names-the-port-a-proc-waits-on}}
 #:seon.task{:name "no-timeout-in-the-proc-loop",
             :plan
             "What exists (flow/impl.clj proc): a :compute step is futurized and .get with compute-timeout-ms 5000; past it the step throws TimeoutException to the error chan, its message is lost, and the step keeps running on the pool. finish-outputs! bounds each output put at stop by 1000 ms and drops past it. Both are clocks standing in for a dependency (docs/clocks.md#if-nothing-has-changed-we-do-nothing). Correct: a :compute step is awaited (the loop's fiber parks on the future); a slow step is visible through ping (a park site of {:computing since}) and is a fact if anyone asks, not an exception.",
             :kind :work,
             :proofs
             #{jolt.flow-test/a-long-compute-step-completes-and-loses-nothing}}
 #:seon.task{:name "update-reports-a-message-a-stopped-proc-held",
             :plan
             "Reproduction (prototype e3_failure): update! restarting a proc whose state held a failed message lost that message, and the report said :lost {} (only dropped in-chans are counted). Correct: a proc's ::flow/stop transition may answer messages it held under ::flow/held, which update! puts back at the front of the new proc's in-chan (or counts in :lost when the in-port is gone).",
             :kind :work,
             :proofs
             #{jolt.flow-test/a-restart-keeps-the-held-message}}
 {:seon.task/name "update-is-one-owners-message"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps (root 2026-11-12, not urgent). Jolt's flow/update! (lane F2) serializes updates with a ReentrantLock around apply!, which stops and starts procs: effects a swap! retry would repeat, so a compare-and-set cannot replace it. The Flow way is one owner fiber of the graph taking update requests on a channel, applying each in order and answering on the request's promise-chan; update! puts and waits on that answer, and the lock goes. Same answers and ordering as today; gates flow and remotefork stay green, and a gate shows two concurrent update! fns compose with no lock held. See `docs/forks.md#jolt`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.flow-test/concurrent-updates-compose-through-the-owner}}
 {:seon.task/name "update-costs-what-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps (jolt-flow 4). flow/update! fingerprints by identity and replans only touched procs, but any change of :conns rebuilds the whole conn-map and routes (stdlib/clojure/core/async/flow/impl.clj apply!), so adding n children one by one is O(n^2). Measured with test/chez/flow-update-test.clj (no make target, so no gate today): 1,000 procs, adding one 1.4-2.1 ms wall. Estimate the lower bound (the touched coords' routes only), measure ticks at n=10/100/1000, patch routes for touched coords only, and make flow-update-test a gate. See `docs/forks.md#jolt`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.flow-test/adding-a-proc-costs-what-it-touches}}
 {:seon.task/name "report-drops-are-counted"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps (root's ruling 2026-11-12). ::flow/report is status and may drop (a sliding buffer, as upstream), but each drop is counted where ping shows it. Jolt's channels are Scheme records (host/chez/java/async.ss async-chan) whose sliding and dropping buffers drop silently: add a drop count to the channel (ac-qdrop-oldest! and the dropping branch), read by a clojure.core.async extension, and ping's report includes the report chan's count. A gate: 150 reports with nobody reading, ping shows 50 dropped. See `docs/forks.md#jolt`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.flow-test/a-flows-report-drops-are-counted-in-ping}}]
